    [
     1,
     "<div style=\"word-wrap:break-word\"><div>Folks,</div><div>  What would be the best way to plot the ecdf of each of these columns in a single chart?</div><div><br></div><div>How about if I want to put each ecdf in its own panel in some kind of 3x3 display with all the x and y limits the same?</div><div><br></div><div>What if I want to reverse the direction of the ecdf, sometimes this is called an exceedance chart.</div><div><br></div><div>Thanks for your time,</div><div>KW</div><div><br></div><div>structure(list(X10..400 = c(-18.1392534702245, 14.1246921733071, </div><div>20.105156515452, -10.0293056051522, 79.9780787509287, -5.79954954730988, </div><div>-11.9427084290315, -8.58958279843375, -21.2841326927118, -15.5914374283893, </div><div>20.2076342432622, -48.2013769264652, 15.5326574147055, -10.8033879714582, </div><div>-1.51607248432964, -8.03592971988138, 18.7819921838991, 5.20486855055492, </div><div>20.3417197605472, 6.48074165849215), X25..400 = c(0, 21.7745681446661, </div><div>37.1672350655981, -37.061948148269, 209.966637661476, -27.1403695719935, </div><div>-41.4974948557998, -33.7028629342175, -62.6844768016134, -49.8652573637297, </div><div>37.4338831935111, 0, 25.368648861514, -38.8603258100308, -16.9287160882878, </div><div>-32.406163053123, 33.7331043535427, -0.57135118486957, 37.7829224627037, </div><div>2.58029152617291), X40..400 = c(0, 0, 44.8008122592196, 0, 357.912613242532, </div><div>-55.1724253924943, -76.1701225424655, -64.8316181174807, 0, 0, </div><div>0, 0, 25.7495212179866, -72.3503360259223, -39.9384393943225, </div><div>-62.9311974984053, 0, 0, 45.803536773478, -10.1536971610759), </div><div>    X10..600 = c(-27.2088802053368, 21.1870382599607, 30.157734773178, </div><div>    -15.0439584077284, 119.967118126393, -8.69932432096482, -17.9140626435473, </div><div>    -12.8843741976507, -31.9261990390677, -23.3871561425839, </div><div>    30.3114513648934, -72.3020653896979, 23.2989861220582, -16.2050819571873, </div><div>    -2.274