     3,
     "\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">Folks,</font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">  What would be the best way to plot the ecdf of\r\neach of these columns in a single chart?</font>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">How about if I want to put each ecdf in its own panel\r\nin some kind of 3x3 display with all the x and y limits the same?</font>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">What if I want to reverse the direction of the ecdf, sometimes\r\nthis is called an exceedance chart.</font>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">Thanks for your time,</font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">KW</font>\r\n<br>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">structure(list(X10..400 = c(-18.1392534702245, 14.1246921733071,\r\n</font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">20.105156515452, -10.0293056051522, 79.9780787509287,\r\n-5.79954954730988, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">-11.9427084290315, -8.58958279843375, -21.2841326927118,\r\n-15.5914374283893, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">20.2076342432622, -48.2013769264652, 15.5326574147055,\r\n-10.8033879714582, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">-1.51607248432964, -8.03592971988138, 18.7819921838991,\r\n5.20486855055492, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">20.3417197605472, 6.48074165849215), X25..400 = c(0, 21.7745681446661,\r\n</font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">37.1672350655981, -37.061948148269, 209.966637661476,\r\n-27.1403695719935, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">-41.4974948557998, -33.7028629342175, -62.6844768016134,\r\n-49.8652573637297, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">37.4338831935111, 0, 25.368648861514, -38.8603258100308,\r\n-16.9287160882878, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">-32.406163053123, 33.7331043535427, -0.57135118486957,\r\n37.7829224627037, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">2.58029152617291), X40..400 = c(0, 0, 44.8008122592196,\r\n0, 357.912613242532, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">-55.1724253924943, -76.1701225424655, -64.8316181174807,\r\n0, 0, </font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3\">0, 0, 25.7495212179866, -72.3503360259223, -39.9384393943225,\r\n</font>\r\n<br><font size=\"3